D. Sivakumar and B. N. Raghunandan
Department of Aerospace Engineering, Indian Institute of Science, Bangalore 560 012, India
~Received 19 September 1997; accepted 3 March 1998!
In the context of coaxial swirl injectors used in liquid bipropellant rockets, a detailed experimental study has been made on the mutual interaction between thin coaxial, conical liquid sheets. As the flow rates are increased, the sheets move close to each other and merge at a definite flow condition. The presence of the inner jet influences the behavior of the outer jet, even before merging. The merging and separation processes exhibit hysteresis. This leads to drastic variations in spray characteristics depending on the approach to the hysteresis regime. Many of these features as well as those on the movement of the contact point can be traced to changes occurring in the static pressure in the cavity between the two sheets. © 1998 American Institute of Physics. @S1070-6631~98!03106-7#
I. INTRODUCTION
Liquid–liquid coaxial swirl atomizers are widely used in liquid propellant rocket engines to take advantage of their higher mixing efficiency within a short length of the combustion chamber. The atomizer is comprised of an inner orifice and an outer orifice to release the oxidizer and fuel sprays, respectively, into the combustion chamber. In this atomizer configuration, the jets are released in the form of thin coaxial conical liquid sheets. Merging of the liquid conical sheets and the subsequent mixing of the two jets are essential for the atomization process. In actual working conditions of an engine, the liquid sheets merge and mix with each other very close to the exit of the orifices. Under low flow rate conditions, the two liquid sheets are separated from each other, as seen in Fig. 1~a!. A gradual increase in the liquid flow rate through the orifices induces the liquid sheets to interact and merge with each other at some particular flow conditions and result in a single conical jet, as shown in Fig. 1~b!. A further increase in the liquid flow rate retains the sheets continuously in the merged form. Conversely, a gradual decrease in the flow rate from the merged flow rate condition leads to the separation of liquid sheets at a definite low flow condition. This phenomenon of merging and separation of the liquid sheets assumes importance while throttling is effected in an engine. Detailed information on such an interaction of the liquid sheets is very important for any improvement in the throttling performance characteristics of an atomizer. There appears to be very little work on a liquid–liquid jet interaction that involves several interesting phenomena. In the present experimental work we investigate the physical process underlying the merging and separation behavior of the liquid conical sheets formed from a coaxial swirl atomizer, while variation in flow conditions are effected. The role of geometrical configuration of the atomizer in the phenomenon is also delineated. A large number of theoretical and experimental studies have been made on the jet interaction, with many variations
like the interaction of a jet with nearby surfaces, the interaction of dual and multiple jets, the interaction of equal and unequal buoyant plumes, the turbulent interaction of concentric jets in the absence and presence of the swirl effect, the interaction of an annular air jet on a central liquid jet, etc.1–17 Many of these investigations deal mainly with the flow features arising out of the jet interaction with either a solid surface or an adjacent jet through the entrainment process.1–10 In the jet–jet interaction dealing with gaseous jets,5–10 the jets merge at a smaller distance from the exit plane by bending under a mutual influence and exhibit the behavior of a single jet after the merging point. In this case the flow field can be classified into three distinct regions: the converging region, the merging region, and the combined region. A subatmospheric zone is established between the jets in the converging region, owing to the mutual entrainment process. It seems possible to control the merging point through the controlled supply of fluid into this subatmospheric zone to compensate for the amount of fluid entrained by the jet, which could prevent the drop in static pressure inside the zone.9 It is also shown that this subatmospheric region is influenced strongly by the flow conditions of the individual jets.8,10 Ko and Kwan11 have obtained more information about the converging and merging regions through
turbulence measurements. Dahm et al.13 and Rehab et al.14 have provided further detailed information about the nearfield interaction behavior of different mixing layers by means of flow visualization through laser-induced fluorescence technique. Recently more attention has been paid to study the influence of jet interaction on the spray characteristics coaxial
atomizers.15–17 The influence of the liquid–gas interaction on the disintegration behavior of the central liquid jet has been examined by Mayer and Krulle15 experimentally and compared with numerical simulation. It is reported that the liquid jet turbulence is responsible for the initiation of the wave pattern, whereas the flowing ambient gas is responsible for the eventual atomization of the liquid jet. However, the in
