II. EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURE
A. Coaxial atomizer details
Experiments have been conducted by generating coaxial conical liquid sheets using specifically designed coaxial swirl
atomizers with water as the experimental fluid. The schematic of the coaxial swirl atomizer with some principal dimensions is shown in Fig. 2. The contraction ratio ~the ratio of swirl chamber diameter to the orifice inlet diameter! for the inner nozzle geometry is 8:1 and those of outer nozzle geometry are 10.6:1 and 7.7:1 for the two different orifice diameters, do52.7 and 3.7 mm, respectively. The orifice diameter of the inner atomizer, di , is 1.0 mm and the outer diameter of the inner atomizer orifice is 1.95 mm. The need for providing more length to the inner orifice arises from the practical requirement of establishing undisturbed outer swirling flow between the two convergent walls ~see Fig. 2!. In the case of pressure swirl atomizers, the flowing liquid stream always remains in contact with the outer wall of the orifice due to the centrifugal effect. Hence, any marginal variation in orifice length is unlikely to influence the jet characteristics significantly, except for the marginal rise in frictional losses. This would reflect in the discharge coefficient without much altering the sheet formation outside. So any minor alteration of these geometrical parameters is not expected to modify the observed phenomena during the liquidliquid sheet interaction reported in the following sections. Extensive care has been exercised to ensure that the orifices are coaxial and coplanar. Any misalignment between the orifices can affect the symmetry and swirling motion of the conical jets and, hence, the subsequent interaction between them. A provision has been made in the atomizer to place both inner and outer orifices coaxially through an adjustment of the outer orifice plate. Before each experimental run, the concentricity of the orifices is confirmed by examining the exit plane using a microscope having a magnification factor 103. The liquid flow rate is controlled by fine needle valves and monitored with standard analog pressure gauges having a resolution of 0.5 psi. 