B. Visualization
The still photographic technique has been used to visualize the structure of the spray. A 35 mm still camera is used to take color and black and white photographs with a shutter speed of 1/15 s using 400 ASA films. A plane of symmetry of the jets is illuminated using a thin collimated sheet of light of thickness approximately 1 mm generated from a source placed at right angles to the line of sight of the camera. A sodium-based fluorescent dye was added to the experimental liquid to improve the contrast in the pictures. These photographs clearly delineate the contours of both the conical jets, disintegration and contact regions, as well as the merging and separation conditions of the liquid sheets, which provide the primary data and observations for the present study. A backlighting technique has been used for low shutter speed (1/1000 s) to obtain high-contrast pictures. Additional information on the jet interaction is obtained through 16 mm cine photography ~64 frames/s! using a backlighting technique and also through visual observation of the sheets by illuminating the spray with a strobe light. Quantitative data from these photographs are obtained using a 16 mm picture analyzer and a microfilm reader with a magnification factor of 17.53 .
C. Drop size measurement
Drop size distributions have been measured using the Malvern mastersizer X instrument, which works on the Fraunhofer forward diffraction principle. The size distribution is obtained from the scattered light intensity. This instrument provides the line of sight size distribution, mean drop size ~SMD, Sauter mean diameter! and liquid volume concentration. The principle of operation of the instrument is described in Ref. 25.
