D. Merging and separation behavior of the liquid sheets
In the case of straight jets without a swirling effect,5–10 the merging of jets is inevitable at all flow conditions, with minor changes in the flow features. In contrast, the merging of conical liquid sheets occur at definite flow conditions of inner and outer jets. It is to be noted that, with changes in the flow conditions, the spray characteristics of the individual jets, like the spray cone angle, disintegration behavior of liquid sheets are significantly altered, as mentioned in the previous sections, which could also influence the merging behavior between them.
1. Transition process from the separated to the merged state
The merging process of liquid sheets is accomplished through a well-behaved rapid transition process from the separated to merged state, which occurs at a specific Rei for a particular Reo . This inner flow condition is referred to as the merging flow condition for that particular Reo . This transition process is illustrated in Fig. 9 through cine photographs for a typical Reo . Note that the flow conditions have been held unchanged during the transition process. The liquid sheets initially make contact at a point quite distant from the exit orifice plane, as observed in the second and third frame of Fig. 9. As soon as the contact is established, the liquid sheet contact point or the merging point moves very rapidly toward the exit orifice plane, as seen in the succeeding photographs of Fig. 9. At the end of the transition process, a final steady-state merging distance from the exit orifice plane, Xmp , is reached, as shown in the last frame of Fig. 9. A further analysis of cine films reveals a slight oscillation of the merging point after the transition process. This phenomenon is under investigation and therefore has not been discussed further in this paper. Just as the contact between the sheets is established, the air between the liquid sheets is entrained out rapidly through
