e physical features of the phenomenon in detail, as the phenomenon is of no interest to their investigation. Thus, in a liquid–liquid coaxial swirl atomizer, the jets could be in any of the three regimes as shown in Fig. 10. In regime I, which occurs at higher Rei , the jets are always in a
merged state and form a single conical jet with the liquid sheet contact region close to the exit of the orifice. In contrast, the jets are always in the separated state in regime II at lower Rei . In regime III, the jets are in either a merged or separated state depending on how the corresponding flow conditions are attained. If the inner jet flow rate is in a decreasing mode, then the jets will be in merged state, otherwise they will be separate.
