IV. CONCLUSIONS
The interaction of thin conical liquid sheets formed from liquid–liquid coaxial swirl atomizers are studied using water and water with added surfactant as the experimental fluids. The conical sheets are visualized by illuminating a section of the jets by a collimated sheet of a light and backlighting technique. A fluorescent dye is mixed with the experimental fluid to increase the contrast of the spray contour. The major conclusions of the study are the following. ~1! The presence of an inner jet in the core of the outer jet alters its spray parameters like the spray width, the disintegration distance of the liquid sheet, the tulip length, etc., even before the merging of liquid sheets occurs. The pressure variation between the liquid sheets is inferred to be primarily responsible for this phenomenon. ~2! The transition process from a separated to a merged state is very rapid, depending on the flow conditions. The separation of liquid sheets also exhibits similar behavior. ~3! The process of merging and separation of the two conical liquid sheets exhibits a hysteresis. The extent of hysteresis could be controlled by the inertia and/or swirl intensity of the liquid sheets. ~4! During the merging process, the liquid sheets come in contact at a point farther downstream of the exit orifice
